#speculation(title: [Histamine-Orexin Arousal Bridge and the H1-Blocker Sedation Threshold])[
Histaminergic and orexinergic wake systems interact directly: orexin neurons act on monoaminergic targets — including histaminergic tuberomammillary neurons — to maintain arousal @Sakurai2021OrexinMonoamine, and both systems are canonical wake-promoting neuromodulators @Jones2020ArousalCircuits. Wake-promoting neuromodulators (histamine, orexin, norepinephrine) are further tied to sleep quality and glymphatic clearance @Pedersen2026WakeNeuromodulators.

Applied to MCAS: this bridge is not direct, and the compartment question is contested inside this corpus — the H3 hypothesis treats systemically released mast cell histamine as brain-active (ch18 sec-09), while other chapters state that histamine crosses the blood-brain barrier, and this speculation instead assumes peripheral mast cell histamine does not freely reach central histaminergic receptors, so that any contribution would require an indirect route (for example vagal or immune signalling to the tuberomammillary nucleus) that is not yet evidenced. If such a route exists, episodic mast cell histamine surges could transiently add to the central arousal load that contributes to the "wired but tired" mismatch — high arousal alongside the fatigue and non-restorative sleep that impaired clearance is proposed to produce @Pedersen2026WakeNeuromodulators. By symmetry, the sedation threshold of an H1 blocker — the dose at which central H1 blockade first emerges — could index a patient's central histamine arousal load: a lower threshold would mark a larger compensatory histaminergic drive, a higher one a smaller reserve — the same direction the compensatory-arousal speculation predicts (@spec:histamine-compensatory-arousal). The orexin-histamine interaction is established in animals and in reviews of human circuitry; no study has measured a histamine arousal load or an H1-blocker sedation threshold in ME/CFS (translation gap). (Certainty: 0.35)

_Falsifiable prediction:_ A pharmacodynamic measure of the H1-blocker sedation threshold (dose at which objective vigilance first falls) correlates with a marker of central histaminergic or orexinergic tone (for example cerebrospinal-fluid orexin-A or histamine), and ME/CFS patients with a "wired but tired" profile show a lower threshold — an objectively larger vigilance drop at a given dose — than those without it, consistent with a larger compensatory drive.

*Consequence:* The sedation threshold would become a bedside readout of central histamine arousal load, giving "wired but tired" a specific neuromodulator correlate. This is a research measure, not a basis for dosing antihistamines.
] <spec:histamine-orexin-arousal-bridge>
